<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Release Documentation Reliability"
  summary="Improved release notes quality, documentation accuracy auditing, and changelog automation to reduce customer-facing documentation defects."
  date="2024 - 2026"
  role="Documentation Owner / Quality Lead"
  tags={["Release Engineering", "Quality", "Docs Accuracy", "AI-Assisted Review"]}
>

<h2>Problem</h2>

<p>Documentation quality issues were creating friction at multiple levels:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Release notes</strong> were overly technical, exposing backend implementation details that confused customers instead of explaining impact</li>
  <li><strong>Legacy content leakage</strong>: the platform had migrated from a "Classic" editor to a new Canvas-based builder, but several documentation pages still contained Classic-era content (screenshots, terminology, procedures) that no longer matched the product</li>
  <li><strong>Incomplete operator coverage</strong>: documentation for condition builders listed only 6 of 9 supported comparison operator categories, with DateTime, Object, and String List operators entirely missing</li>
  <li><strong>Changelog automation gaps</strong>: release/changelog workflows occasionally failed silently due to missing tags or temporary branch issues, leaving documentation stale after connector releases</li>
</ul>

<h2>My Role</h2>

<p>I owned release documentation quality across the product, including release notes authoring, documentation accuracy auditing against the codebase, and changelog automation reliability.</p>

<h2>What I Did</h2>

<h3>Release Notes Quality</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Established a structured format for release notes: enhancement and bug fix sections, grouped by product area</li>
  <li>Rewrote release notes to focus on customer-visible impact rather than backend implementation details</li>
  <li>Added verification checks to ensure affected version accuracy and appropriate severity language</li>
</ol>

<h3>Documentation Accuracy Auditing</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Audited documentation pages against the live codebase using AI-assisted code analysis to compare what the docs described vs. what the product actually implemented</li>
  <li>Identified that two major documentation pages ("Flows and Conditions" and "If/Else Conditions") were serving Classic-era content in the current product documentation section</li>
  <li>Verified that more accurate, Canvas-based documentation already existed elsewhere in the doc tree, and coordinated the cleanup to remove the stale pages</li>
  <li>Documented the full comparison operator gap (3 missing categories, 10+ missing operators) and confirmed coverage in the correct documentation pages</li>
</ol>

<h3>Changelog Automation Hardening</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Diagnosed failure modes in the release/changelog automation workflows</li>
  <li>Fixed missing tag behavior that broke release tracking</li>
  <li>Added fallback handling for missing changelog files</li>
  <li>Ensured release/publish flows remain non-fatal when changelog steps fail</li>
</ol>

<h3>Proactive Documentation Fixes</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Identified a CSS issue in the marketplace connector details page where changelog headings rendered with an inverted visual hierarchy (h2 appeared smaller than h3)</li>
  <li>Traced the root cause to the SCSS styling and proposed the fix</li>
  <li>Documented JSONata quoting rules (SPT-52232) across three documentation pages after a production issue revealed that customers were using unsupported single-quote syntax</li>
</ol>

<h2>Constraints</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Auditing documentation accuracy against a large codebase (Angular, TypeScript, SCSS) without engineering resources dedicated to documentation</li>
  <li>Release notes timelines were tight, often requiring turnaround within hours of ticket resolution</li>
  <li>Classic-to-Canvas migration was ongoing, making it difficult to determine which content was current vs. legacy without codebase verification</li>
</ul>

<h2>Outcomes</h2>

<h3>Customer Impact</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Customers receive release notes that clearly describe what changed and why, without confusing backend details</li>
  <li>Removed stale Classic-era content that was misleading users following current product documentation</li>
  <li>Filled comparison operator documentation gaps, giving users complete reference material</li>
  <li>Proactive JSONata quoting guidance reduced preventable user errors in transformation expressions</li>
</ul>

<h3>Quality Impact</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Fewer than 20 TechPubs JIRA bugs/stories in the last six months, reflecting improved documentation quality</li>
  <li>Changelog automation failures no longer silently break documentation currency</li>
  <li>Established a repeatable pattern for auditing documentation accuracy against the codebase</li>
</ul>

<h2>AI-Assisted Workflow</h2>

<p>AI-assisted analysis was critical to several parts of this work:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Codebase-to-docs comparison</strong>: AI navigated the Angular/TypeScript source to identify what comparison operators, condition types, and UI behaviors actually existed vs. what was documented</li>
  <li><strong>Root cause tracing</strong>: AI traced the CSS rendering issue for changelog headings by reading the component SCSS and identifying the inverted heading styles</li>
  <li><strong>Content gap identification</strong>: AI compared Classic and Canvas documentation side-by-side to pinpoint where legacy content had leaked into current pages</li>
</ul>

<h2>Lessons Learned</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Documentation accuracy auditing against the codebase should be a regular practice, not a reactive one; legacy content leakage is invisible until a customer or team member notices</li>
  <li>Release notes that expose backend details erode customer trust; the "what changed for me" framing is always better than "what the engineer fixed"</li>
  <li>AI-assisted code analysis makes codebase-to-docs auditing practical for documentation teams that do not have dedicated engineering support</li>
</ul>

